{"module": "InterviewGraphBuilder", "question_preview": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "Hello, I'm Dr. Priya Raman, an energy economics and policy analyst at the International Energy Economics Institute. My work focuses on the economics of the clean energy transition: the falling costs of solar, wind, and batteries, carbon pricing, subsidies, and green industrial policy. I'm especially interested in which policy tools deliver the most emissions reduction per dollar, and how to spread the cost fairly across households and regions.\n\nSolar, wind, and battery costs have fallen dramatically over the past decade, and in many markets they now beat fossil fuels on levelized cost. Yet deployment in many regions still seems to depend heavily on subsidies and policy support. **Where does the cost advantage of clean technologies actually break down in practice, and what are the biggest hidden costs, such as grid integration, financing, or intermittency, that headline cost figures miss?**", "type": "text"}], "timestamp": "2026-11-10T14:51:41.003317Z", "level": "info", "event": "Question generated successfully"}
{"module": "InterviewGraphBuilder", "question_preview": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "Hello, I'm Daniel Reyes, an analyst working on a piece about the practical side of the clean energy transition. Thanks for making time, expert.\n\n\"Clean energy and low-carbon technologies\" is a broad topic, so I'd like to start where your work sits: the engineering that keeps the lights on as variable renewables take a bigger share of the grid.\n\n**When a grid moves from, say, 30% to 70-80% wind and solar, what is the bottleneck that surprises people most? Is it generation, storage, transmission, interconnection queues, or something else entirely? Could you give a specific example of where you've seen it show up in practice?**", "type": "text"}], "timestamp": "2026-11-10T14:51:41.023619Z", "level": "info", "event": "Question generated successfully"}
